Date: Fri, 26 Nov 1993 23:53:54 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

There has been an interesting thread recently on alt.politics.datahighway,
concerning the problem of rewarding authors in a world of machine readable
books, anonymity, etc. It was started by someone who argued that copyright
was unenforcable in that context, and suggested a government run archive
for all published works, with authors paid (out of tax money) a fee that
was proportional to the number of people who accessed their work and an
increasing function of the size of the work. I thought people on this list
might be interested in my solution to the problem of copyright in such a
world. I do not know how much of it has been proposed before by others. The
following is a slightly edited version of what I just posted.

------

After thinking about it, I believe I have a solution to the problem of
copyrights in a world of widespread encryption, anonymous remailers, and
associated technology. It works as follows:

I am an author. I offer my work for sale on the following terms. The
purchaser agrees to pay me for a license which does not include the right
to pass on the work, in machine readable form, to anyone else who will then
make it available on line. He further agrees to pay me damages if any copy
of his copy is made available on line.

Enforcing this contract involves two problems--determining whether it has
been violated and collecting damages. To solve the first, I provide every
purchaser with his own custom version of the work, and send it to him
encrypted with his public key--thus guaranteeing that if it gets out (in
decrypted form) it is his fault.

By a custom version of the work, I mean a version which has been altered in
a way that makes it possible to identify future copies of it. A simple
version of this idea has been used by  encyclopedias for a long time. They
put in a few bogus entries, to make it possible to prove that a competing
encyclopedia was copied from them. In my version, the identification is
different for each customer. It could include features such as occasional
deliberate misspellings, a particular pattern of variant sentences,
messages encoded in the low bits of graphic images, et multae caetera.
Enough is put in so that someone who copies and tries to eliminate all of
the special features is likely to fail.

What about collecting damages? In the world I am describing, someone can
make an enforcable contract in at least two different ways. First, he can
do business under his true name and live in a jurisdiction that enforces
contracts. Second, he can post a bond with some reliable third party, with
part of the bond forfeiting if he is adjudged guilty of contract violation.
The former method puts him under public law, the latter under the private
law (i.e. arbitration) used by the reliable third party. I, as an author,
will send my work only to people who have made it possible for me to
collect damages from them if they let it out.

Note that in such a world, there will be a market for reliable third
parties and ways of producing them. One way is to do business under your
true name in a jurisdiction that enforces contracts. Another is to build up
enough reputational capital (under your public name) so that it is more
profitable to remain reliable.

A problem that may occur to some of you is the risk of opportunistic
behavior by the author. Suppose I anonymously send copies of several marked
copies I have sold to several pirate archives, they put them on line, then
I claim that my customers have let the work out and sue them. There are two
solutions to this problem:

A. Legal rules such that the damages minus my litigation costs are less
than the cost to me of the work getting out, but the damages plus my
customer's litigation costs are more than what he can sell his copy to the
pirate for. If necessary, the difference between the cost to him of losing
the litigation and the benefit to me of winning it can be made larger by
having part of his damage payment go to a charity or other third party.

B. The use of a trusted intermediary. I send my work to the trusted
intermediary. It prepares the custom copies and sends them out, keeping
records so that it can determine whose original a copy was made from.
Presumably there would be firms in the business of doing this.

A possible objection to this is that a reader is risking a large liability
judgement if his copy somehow gets out. But under current institutions,
every driver takes similar risks. Drivers are insured--and readers could
be. Also, in a world of widespread encryption, keeping things from
accidentally getting out should be relatively simple.

This set of institutions has several attractive features. It enforces
copyright in a world where parties can make themselves immune to law,
either by anonymity or by choosing to live in a jurisdiction that does not
enforce the law in question. It does so by restricting access to the work
to people who have chosen to put themselves under law--and making them
responsible if they pass it on. It does not depend on any particular legal
regime. It makes it in the interest of jurisdictions to enforce contracts
on their residents, since doing so makes them more attractive places to
live--no need to post bond. And it does everything in a completely
decentralized fashion, and one that takes advantage of private as well as
public law.

Comments?

