Date: Sat, 27 Nov 93 19:51:54 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

Unfortunately, David Friedman's scheme of distributing N different
plaintexts, each varying in some degree so as to "tag" the texts, has
been studied before and found wanting.

For example, two texts may be acquired, by colluders, who then
determine the differing sections. By merging the two texts, the
initial author knows only that one or the other, or both, of the two
recipients stole the text, etc. When N such collusions occur, the "tag
bits" can also be excised and the resulting text distributed.

(The N sourcetexts may not exhaust the tag bit space, of course. That
is, the original author may be able to narrow down the leaker to one
of the N sourcetext holders, but not further. Maybe enough not to sell
to anyone one of these N folks, but surely not convincing enough to
successfully prosecute them. Except as a herd.)

As the changes get large enough such that each of M recipients gets
what appears to be a custom text, then the comparison/XOR approach
begins to fall apart. Of course, giving M recipients a text that
differs substantially from the others and yet is still readable,
itself becomes a tough problem.

--Tim May

