>begins to fall apart. Of course, giving M recipients a text that
>differs substantially from the others and yet is still readable,
>itself becomes a tough problem.

I had considered the strategy of using two or more texts to try to spot the
identifying variations, although perhaps I was too optimistic about ways of
controlling it. It seemed to me that there were probably enough clever ways
of varying texts so that such a method would not work reliably--and, of
course, the more variants the pirate is using as inputs, the more people
will owe damages if they get caught.

My simplest scheme is for the author, as he writes the work, to
occasionally note places where two alternative versions of a sentence are
equally good, and record both versions. Two hundred such provide  2**200 =
aprox 10**60 variant texts. Any two texts will agree with each other on
about 100 of the sentences and disagree on about 100, so a pirate working
from two texts will still have 100 of the tag sentences unidentified. If
the pirate gave both versions for the identified one, it would be easy to
spot both sources--there are 10**30 different patterns for the 100
unidentified. If, as seems more sensible, he randomly chooses one of the
alternatives for each identified sentence, it gets harder--I have not done
any actual calculations. I had supposed that combining this scheme with
four or five others would be sufficient--am I wrong? Note that it is not
necessary to make piracy impossible--just expensive.

Note also that it is not clear how many variant texts have to go out. Many
customers may be libraries, which make their single variant available to
many users in some read-only mode (for example, in the library).

