I suppose a book/article published in such a regime would consist of
the item plus a unique serial number concatenated together and signed
with the author's public key. I'm assuming, for the moment, that
steganography here would not be worth the added expense. Everyone
*knows* that there would be serial numbers "embedded" somewhere in the
text, and unless a different method was used for each book (unlikely)
there would soon be "cracking" tools for giving a copy of the article
new "serial numbers." In this case any cracked article that someone
decided to upload would not be verified with the author's public key.

People would not read those out of simple self-defense against
fraud. Unless the reader has seen a verified copy of the book through
other channels, and likely paid for it, he or she might not know that
the chapter titled "Julius Caesar's Visit to Iceland:
Crypto-Anarchy That Worked" wasn't in the original work.


