Date: Sun, 28 Nov 1993 12:27:15 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

Tim May:

> I did not mean to dismiss David's idea, only to note a point of attack
> that others have also found (I seem to recall Derek Zahn and Perry
> Metzger joining in on a thread along these lines some months back,
> perhaps 6-8 months ago....

Well, I don't recall that, but then again my long-term
memory duration has shrunk to a couple of months, I fear,
so it's possible (when it shrinks to the same duration as
my short term memory, that'll be a Singularity, I guess).

Still, the analysis shouldn't be too difficult.  Off the
cuff, I'll start.  Correction and elaboration are welcome.

Let's start by saying there are some number N of "atomic
change" sites in the text -- each point an atomic
change can be made from a base reference text.  For
the purposes of this analysis we'll say that an atomic
change is either present or not, though there's no reason
that we couldn't extend it to have multiple "alleles"
for each change (I don't think that would change the
results of this analysis very much).

An atomic change should have the following properties:

* A (simple) transformation program should not be able to
  render the change unrecognizable.  For instance, the
  simplest schemes, involving the placement  of extra  spaces
  between certain  words would not be sufficient because
  a simple filter program could easily strip out all such
  changes.  Similarly, the use of synonyms, spelling errors,
  and patterned syntax alterations are inadequate because
  they are too easily undone.  David's scheme of alternate
  sentence wordings, if carefully done, would probably work
  for many cases.  I will use the following, though, which I
  consider to be more robust to mangling attacks:  an atomic
  change is simply the deletion of some adjective, adverb,
  phrase, or sentence in the text.  A programmed mangler
  is unable to undo such a change because that would require
  the insertion of the correct text, which can't be guessed
  with more than a vanishingly small probability of success.
  Still, it is possible, by randomly deleting parts of the
  text, to create "false positives" by happily deleting
  relevant parts of the text.  Note, however, that such
  a mangler will degrade the quality of the text, making
  the changed copy less desirable to "customers".  Let's say
  that an M-efficient text mangler will make a fraction M of
  the unmade atomic changes appear to be made, while still
  maintaining some sufficient level of text quality.  I suspect
  that M will be quite small (though we can debate this
  subtlety if somebody thinks it's important), so I will omit
  the possibility from the analysis.

* The atomic changes should not overlap or interfere with each
  other.  This is easy.

Now, let's say that each legal copy contains some fraction
F of the atomic changes, chosen at random from the N available (so
N*F atomic changes are made per purchaser).  We can think of the
text's "signature" as being an N-digit binary number containing
ones for the changes made in that text.  For best recognition,
we want to minimize the number of digits that match in two
randomly-generated texts' signatures.  That number will be
N * (2F - 2F^2), which is minimized for F= 0.5, so I'll use
that from now on.  So the signature overlap on average will be
N/2 bits.

We are interested in analyzing some "illegal" text.  Innocent
purchasers will have text signatures that overlap with the
signature of the illegal text in N/2 places, on average.  In
fact, only one in a million will overlap in at least N/2 + 20
places (I think; log2(1000000) == 20)... this gives a simple
relationship between the degree of overlap and the chances of
that overlap happening accidentally.

Now, the question is how well colluders can do.

Maximal-protection collusion strategy:  discover as many of the
atomic changes as they can, and then randomly use or not use
them to create the illegal version so their legal versions
will score equally on the match with the illegal version.

Two colluders can on average discover N/2 of the atomic changes.
After constructing the maximal-protection illegal version,
each of their personal versions will match all of the N/2
undiscovered positions and half of those discovered: 3N/4
positions.  If N > 80, they'll both fail the one-in-a-million
test.

Of more interest is the case of X colluders, and here the math
gets a little more tricky:  Since each pairwise comparison
of a signature reveals N/2 (avg) of the atomic changes,
Z comparisons will reveal N - N/(2^Z) of them.  X colluders
will be able to perform (X^2 - X)/2 comparisons, giving a
truly ugly expression for the number of revealed atomic changes.
MathCad provides the following answers for various numbers of
colluders:

X = 2:    N - N/2
X = 3:    N - N/8
X = 4:    N - N/64
X = 5:    N - N/1024
X = 6:    N - N/32768
X = 10:   N - N/35184372088832
X = 100:  N - underflow

Now, if B atomic changes are revealed, the optimal strategy
will give an illegal version's signature that overlaps with
B/2 + (N-B) bits of each colluder's text's signature (B/2 ==
randomly chosen from those discovered; N-B == number left
undiscovered).  For the one-in-a-million test to catch
the culprits, 

  B/2 + (N-B) = N/2 + 20
  N = B + 40

This lets us determine how many atomic change points are required
to to protect from X colluders with that level of confidence:

X = 2:   N = 80
X = 3:   N = 320
X = 4:   N = 2560
X = 5:   N = 40960
X = 6:   N = 1310720
X = 10:  N = 1.4 x 10^15

CONCLUSION:

The Friedman scheme seems possible for protection against
five or fewer colluders; for more than five colluders, the
number of changes that the author would have to provide is
unreasonably large.

derek

