From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Copyright under Crypto Anarchy

I worked through some rough numerical calculations last night while trying
to fall asleep, and it looks to me as though Tim is correct. My method is
unworkable in the form I proposed it. If the approximations I used are
legitimate, I can send out thousands of copies of a book with 200 variant
sentences in it (each can be in either of two versions) and spot who
cheated if the pirate bases his release on three or fewer texts. But if he
uses as many as ten texts, figuring out which ones they are appears to be
impossible with any reasonable number of variant sentences and similar
devices. So all the pirate has to do is create ten pseudonyms and have each
order a copy of the book.

Tim writes: 
>That
>is, the original author may be able to narrow down the leaker to one
>of the N sourcetext holders, but not further. Maybe enough not to sell
>to anyone one of these N folks, but surely not convincing enough to
>successfully prosecute them. Except as a herd.)

I do not understand this. If the author can identify the whole collection
of holders who leaked, that is sufficient--he collects damages from all of
them. Is that what you mean by prosecuting them as a herd? If so, what is
wrong with it? 

My calculations, unfortunately, suggest that he will not be able to so
identify them for any significantly large N. The pirate simply identifies
the variant sentences (by looking for disagreements among his sourcetexts)
and creates his text using some combination of majority rule (to prevent
the author from identifying the source by putting a unique variant sentence
in each version) and coin flipping. With N=10 and each sentence having two
variants with equal frequency, he will miss only 1/1024 of the sentences.
The author then has to find all of the groups of ten of the sourcetexts he
sent out (assuming, for simplicity, that he knows N) which have about
1/1024th of the variants in common with each other and the pirated version.
Unless I have made a mistake, there will be many different such groups, so
identifying the leakers is not practical. If C is the number of copies, we
have C!/((c-10)!10!) groups of ten, or about (C**10)/10!, which is a very
large number. If I had 1024 variant sentences I would be looking for groups
of ten with one variant in common with each other and the pirated
version--which about half of all the groups of ten will have! Even pushing
the number of variants much higher will, I think, be inadequate--quite
aside from the computational cost of checking about 10**20 groups of ten!

I am not, however, willing to give up yet. Several approaches to dealing
with these problems suggest themselves:

1. Use this method for primary distribution of a relatively small number of
copies--as in my library example. The customers then redistribute in some
"read only" mode. This is very unsatisfactory for books--it puts us in the
situation of movies before home video. But it might work for a data base,
say, where the customers are querying it, not reading it. Of course, it
only makes sense in contexts where there is an advantage to having copies
at a hundred sites (libraries) instead of only at one.

2. Sell copies only to people operating under their true names. This does
not solve the problem, but it makes life a little harder for the pirate who
is acquiring sourcetexts, since he has to actually bribe real people.
Perhaps if you can get some of the "bribed" people (or volunteer
contributors of sourcetext) to be plants, there is some way of either
corrupting the pirate copy so it is inferior to real copies, or identifying
the remaining source texts. For example, you create three variants of some
sentences--one of which is terrible. All of your plants and only your
plants use the terrible version, so it outvotes the good version (assuming
at least a third of the texts used are planted), producing a terrible
version for the pirate to distribute. There may be a workable version of
this scheme. Of course, it will have to contend with the pirate's strategy
of using only people he trusts as sources.

3. Make your variations relate to each other. A simple example is an
arithmetic text, with variant numbers in the problems. The computer that
prepares variant versions will match problem versions with answer versions.
A pirate who is preparing his version mechanically, as described above,
will not--leading to unhappy customers of the pirate.

Obviously, this example is too simple to actually work. The pirate either
tells his computer to match problems and answers or has his computer check
for correlations among variants. But a more complicated version might well
work. The trick is to have variant units that are not obviously units, but
that will produce an inferior version of the text if they are not varied
together.

Consider, for example, the distribution of software in machine language
form. The compiler produces ten thousand different versions, all
functionally equivalent, having been compiled from the same source code.
Short of the pirate decompiling--i.e. disassembling and then figuring out
what the assembly language means--this should be an effective way of
tagging a version. I am sure someone has suggested this one in some
context.

Or consider a computer generated video. The variants are things like camera
angles. Or an interactive VR adventure--which is essentially a computer
program distributed in machine code. The pirate can record a single play of
the adventure, or many plays, but I would think that recreating the whole
interconnected structure in this way could be prohibitively difficult. If
he does a mechanical combination of versions of the distributed program,
the hero's name ends up changing every ten minutes or so.

This approach gets us into a problem analogous to the one whose solution
produced public key cryptography--one way transformation. We need ways in
which it is easy for the author to vary his work but hard for the pirate to
identify and undo the variations.

David Friedman
Cornell Law School
