Ironically, I recall almost these exact words being used the last time
this came up, that is, the ease with which pseudonyms could be used to
order the N copies needed to reconstruct the original text.

(An error detection and correction problem, with the unmutated text
being the "signal" or "message" and then various point
mutations--errors--introduced. So long as the errors are sparsely
distributed--high S/N ratio--then comparison of a relatively small
number of texts is sufficient to recover the message. Law of large
numbers, Shannon's coding theorem, etc. I was working on a longer
piece on this in my off-line newsreader, but the recent posts of Derek
Zahn and now David Friedman make this issue fairly moot. I will use my
best pun on you folks, though. In explaining the situation in terms of
atlas makers who try to foil copyright violators by introducing phony
cities, I noted that the XOR of 2 or more atlases allows violator to
simply throw out the XOR hits. Accuracy wins; the practice of
deliberately introducing errors is declining. Ultimately, atlas makers
shrugged.)

