> sent out (assuming, for simplicity, that he knows N) which have about
> 1/1024th of the variants in common with each other and the pirated version.
> Unless I have made a mistake, there will be many different such groups, so
> identifying the leakers is not practical. If C is the number of copies, we

I agree. For any given S/N ratio--that is, for any given point
mutation rate--one can recover the error-free signal by looking at
enough messages. The calculations show this is a relatively small
number of messages (dozens or less) for reasonable point mutation rates.

...
> "read only" mode. This is very unsatisfactory for books--it puts us in the
> situation of movies before home video. But it might work for a data base,
> say, where the customers are querying it, not reading it. Of course, it
> only makes sense in contexts where there is an advantage to having copies
> at a hundred sites (libraries) instead of only at one.

Interestingly, I predicted this as a trend for corporate knowledge--as
in the extremely valuable design methods and rules in a chip
company--in an era of crypto anarchy. Knowledge would be embedded in
"oracular" databases which would be protected against core dumps and
other forms of getting at the direct knowledge. Since a single DAT
tape can hold 80 MB of data stegonagrapically hidden in just the least
significant bits, it is hopeless to try to stop employess from
stealing and then selling information they can directly access. The
"oracle" data base, on the other hand, keeps its knoweldge to itself
and only answers certain questions.

(How many questions one can ask such an agent or oracle to deduce the
complete underlying knowledge, a la "Twenty Questions," is a
fascinating problem unto itself, closely related to this issue of
recovering signals in the presence of noise and to algorithmic
information theory.)

> 3. Make your variations relate to each other. A simple example is an
> arithmetic text, with variant numbers in the problems. The computer that
> prepares variant versions will match problem versions with answer versions.
> A pirate who is preparing his version mechanically, as described above,
