This is an interesting idea. Essentially, this looks like adding
long-range order to the document (correlations) so as to foil point
mutation approaches. Put in terms of the example earlier, "A screaming
arcs across the sky" might be recognizable as not being the long-range
order implicit in Pynchon's writing style. A la the textual analysis
of Marlowe vs. Bacon vs. Shakespeare.

> Consider, for example, the distribution of software in machine language
> form. The compiler produces ten thousand different versions, all
> functionally equivalent, having been compiled from the same source code.
> Short of the pirate decompiling--i.e. disassembling and then figuring out
> what the assembly language means--this should be an effective way of
> tagging a version. I am sure someone has suggested this one in some
> context.

I've heard this proposed as a form of copy protection, but never
actually deployed. Some entrepreneur could perhaps make this a reality.

But of course the changes must be "deep," in the sense of not being
merely point mutations. Otherwise, the XOR methods could quickly find
the point mutations. And any program robust against such changes would
also be vulnerable to "fingerprint smearing" by bootleggers who went
in and replaced chunks of code with the same kind of "functionally
equivalent" chunks. Sauce for the gander and all.

It's almost like foiling CD copying pirates by having every CD have
songs that are different...not so different as to be unlistenable and
not so alike as to be easily corrected. Figuring out how to do this
sounds real tough.

> This approach gets us into a problem analogous to the one whose solution
> produced public key cryptography--one way transformation. We need ways in
> which it is easy for the author to vary his work but hard for the pirate to
> identify and undo the variations.

Sounds like it. A tough problem.

